io_extractor_fixture_wrapper_alias_20260:
Tenth fix in the skip-rate chain -- the single LARGEST recovery of any fix in this session. Checking whether xh (68.5% skip after fixes 1-9) had a second cause beyond the already-confirmed http_server network-mock-server category (which is genuinely unfixable, see the ov/xh notes in fix 8's entry): sampled a broad spread of xh's skips and found test_nested_json.py entirely unresolved, 0/56 tests. Root cause: ```
def run_xh(*args, ...): ...subprocess.run(cmd, ...)...  # plain function, real impl
_run_xh_func = run_xh   # captured BEFORE the name gets shadowed below
@pytest.fixture
def xh():
    \"\"\"docstring\"\"\"
    return _run_xh_func
@pytest.fixture
def run_xh(): ...  # a DIFFERENT fixture, same name, shadows the plain fn
def test_simple_nested_object(xh):
    result = xh('--print=B', '--offline', '--ignore-stdin', ':', 'a[b]=value')
```
_discover_wrapper_names correctly finds 'run_xh' (the plain function directly shells out via subprocess.run). But every test in this file calls `xh(...)`, not `run_xh(...)` -- and 'xh' was never registered as a runner name, because _shells_out()/_discover_wrapper_names only look for a function whose body directly CONTAINS a Call to subprocess/a known runner; a fixture whose entire body is `return _run_xh_func` has NO Call node anywhere in it (a bare Name return), so it was invisible to the existing discovery mechanism even though it is transparently just another name for the exact same real function. Fix: _discover_fixture_wrapper_aliases(tree, path, known_names) resolves ONE hop of simple module-level `alias = name` assignment (`_run_xh_func = run_xh`), so a @pytest.fixture whose body is a bare `return <name>` (after skipping a leading docstring, if present) resolves to a name already known to be a runner; if so, the FIXTURE's own name is registered as an additional alias in extra_run_names for this file. First implementation attempt incorrectly required exactly one body statement (`len(node.body) != 1`) and measured ZERO recovery on real data -- broke immediately because `xh`'s fixture (like nearly every real fixture) has a docstring before the return. Fixed by skipping a leading string-literal ast.Expr docstring before checking body shape; re-measured and confirmed real recovery this time (never trust a fix without measuring the SAME real file before declaring it done -- this session's own recurring discipline caught its own near-miss here). REAL A/B counterfactual: test_nested_json.py alone went from 0/56 to 56/56 examples -- full recovery of an entire file. Across xh's whole directory (one branch): 306->516 examples (+210) of 917 tests. Across the full 6-tool re-scan (3 branches each): xh 318->529 examples, skip rate 68.5%->47.7% -- the single largest recovery of any fix in this chain. Other 5 tools unchanged in this specific fix (the run/fixture name-collision idiom happens to be xh-specific in the sampled branches, though the mechanism is general and applies to any tool using the same pattern -- a plain wrapper function whose name gets shadowed by a same-named fixture elsewhere in the file, with the original captured under an alias for a DIFFERENT fixture to return transparently). 8 new tests in tests/test_determinex_io_extractor_fixture_aliases.py (6 unit tests on _discover_fixture_wrapper_aliases: simple one-hop alias, direct-return-no-assignment variant, unrelated-name rejection, non-fixture-function rejection, multiple-statements-before-return rejection -- never guess past real logic in the fixture body -- and bare `fixture` decorator name; 2 extract_file() integration: end-to-end resolution through the alias, and an unrelated-fixture-of-the-same-shape regression guard confirming the mechanism doesn't over-fire on any bare-return fixture, only ones that actually resolve to a known runner). Full session corpus suite (io_extractor + local_oracle) clean at 160 tests.
